        setup: {
          players: 'One player. Plait is a solo patience game played by a single person; BVS and historical sources describe it as a single-player card game with no opponents or partners.',
          deck: '104 cards (two standard 52-card decks).',
          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Deal 13 cards in a plait (open reserve). Deal 32 cards into 8 tableau piles (4 cards per pile). Place one card above as the first foundation base; the seven other cards of the same rank become foundation bases as they appear. Remainder is stock.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Move all 104 cards to 8 foundation piles, building up in suit from the Base rank (wrapping K to A).',
          gameplay: 'Build tableaus down in sequence and alternating color. Only the top card of each tableau pile and the reserve (plait) is available for play on the foundations. One card or a group of cards in proper sequence may move to another tableau pile. When a tableau is empty, any card or legal sequence may fill it. When no moves are available, turn cards from the stock one at a time; cards from stock may move to tableau or foundations. No redeal.',
          keyRules: [
            'Foundations build up in same suit from base rank, wrapping King to Ace.',
            'Tableau builds down in alternating color; only top cards and plait top are available for foundations.',
            'One card or a legal sequence may move to another tableau; empty tableau accepts any card or legal sequence.',
            'Turn stock one card at a time when no other moves; no redeal.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Free the Plait! The 13 cards in the Plait are your biggest obstacle. Don't use your stock cards or tableau moves if you could instead move a card from the Plait to a foundation. Every card you remove from the Plait increases your tactical options for the rest of the game.",
          intermediate: "Foundation Focus: Since foundations can start on any rank (like an 8), the sequence is non-standard (8-9-10-J-Q-K-A-2-3-4-5-6-7). Pay close attention to the wrap-around. Don't trap an Ace in a tableau pile unless you have already cleared the Kings for all foundations.",
          advanced: "The Alternating Trap: While you build down in alternating colors in the tableau, remember that you must eventually build UP in suit in the foundations. If you create a long Black-Red-Black sequence in the tableau, you might be 'Burying' a Diamond and a Heart that are needed for early foundation building. Use empty tableau columns to 'Break' and 'Sort' your suit sequences whenever possible.",
          tips: [
            "Look ahead at the Plait. If the card after the current one is a high-value Base card, prioritize clearing the current card even if it's not ideal for the foundations.",
            "Keep at least one tableau column empty for as long as possibleâ€”it is your most flexible 'Sorting' zone.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
